1. Disk Space & Log Rotation Management
 The proposed layout uses LVM (Logical Volume Manager) so every functional area is a separate logical volume with its own size cap, combined with logrotate for automatic log lifecycle management. This isolates failure domains and gives an online-resizable safety margin.

 •(root) — 8 GB: only OS binaries and configuration; kept small and stable so it rarely grows.
•var/log — separate 10 GB logical volume (XFS with project quota as a second safety net) 
•home — 15 GB for user files, the most predictable, slow-growing segment.
•opt (applications) and /var/lib (app data) — 14 GB combined, sized for moderate growth with room to spare.
•3 GB left as unallocated LVM free extents — a reserve pool that can be assigned via lvextend.
•logrotate configured with daily rotation, compression (gzip), a per-file maxsize guard, and a 10-rotation,



Q2. Scheduler Optimization (Priority Scheduling)
The solution combines four complementary kernel-level levers rather than one single fix, since each targets a different source of context-switch overhead.
•Scheduler tuning: move latency-critical tasks to SCHED_FIFO/SCHED_RR (real-time policies) via chrt, 
•CPU isolation and affinity: use isolcpus (or cpuset/cset shield) to dedicate specific cores exclusively to real-time tasks.
•Kernel modules: where a hot code path is dominated by syscall/interrupt overhead, 
•Tickless kernel and preemption model: enable CONFIG_NO_HZ_FULL so idle/isolated cores stop taking periodic timer interrupts

Q3. Fast Processing of 1 Million Log Entries
The design splits the file into independent chunks, processes them in parallel with fast streaming tools, and merges results using an algorithm that avoids re-sorting the entire dataset.

•Split the file into 8 (or N = number of CPU cores) roughly equal chunks using split -n l/8, avoiding cutting lines mid-record.
•Process chunks in parallel with xargs -P8 (or GNU parallel), 
•Each chunk is pre-sorted where ordering/grouping is needed, 
•uniq -c or an awk associative array aggregates counts/summaries from the merged stream.
•Final output is written once with buffered I/O rather than many small writes, minimizing disk thrashing.


Q4. Identifying & Controlling a Faulty High-CPU Process

The strategy follows a four-stage pipeline: detect, diagnose, act (with three graduated options), and safeguard/monitor — escalating only as far as necessary.
•Detect & identify: top/htop or ps aux --sort=-%cpu to find the exact PID consuming 90% CPU.
